          "text": "Research doesn't happen in a vacuum-how we come to our research interests is influenced by our experiences in the world, what we value, and what our society values. Our experiences lead us to care about research problems that don't yet have answers. Then we muster interest in our research problem among our supervisors (or dissertation committees), our colleagues, the public, potential participants, and funding sources. Funding sources play a major role in how research expands, shapes, and clarifies what humanity knows. Funding for social science research in the arts has never drawn from a deep well, but the trigger words this administration has recently placed on NIH and NSF funding curtails knowledge about the arts even more, not to mention the fields that have typically received more funding. Take some time at this station to reflect on how these research topic bans shape our understanding of the world."
