      "infoHotspots": [
        {
          "yaw": 2.5057955527321703,
          "pitch": -0.12637124018189816,
          "title": "Audrey: Visualizing Data",
          "text": "<div>When arts organizations (especially museums) and schools make a hybrid form of education, they leverage the resources that informal learning centers like museums have with the structure of formal learning in grade school. But formal (school) and informal (museum) environments have their own identities and constraints that often hinder collaboration.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>This research uses case study methodology with grounded theory analysis, focusing on one unit or case (a particular school and its arts partners) and resulting in a visual model of their process. The process includes the actions, resources, and values that help make school–museum learning happen. While my visual model will eventually become a streamlined abstraction of what goes into this work, I love exploring things in tangible ways like this diorama.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>One question I asked my participants was “How should museums and schools work together?” I wanted to explore what practitioners envision when thinking about what could be. I invite you to do the same here. Please write down or create any element that could be part of school–museum collaboration: an action, a resource, an emotion, a value, a policy, an objective…anything you would like to see in the picture. You may alter this diorama in any way that helps you think about how museums and schools should work together.</div>"
        }
      ]
